1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,843
In this lesson, we'll review sequences and
the notation for adding their terms.

2
00:00:05,967 --> 00:00:11,808
An explicit formula gives any term
directly. a sub n equals n squared plus

3
00:00:11,808 --> 00:00:14,649
one gives two, five, ten, seventeen.

4
00:00:15,099 --> 00:00:20,305
Arithmetic sequences are linear in n;
geometric ones are exponential.

5
00:00:21,433 --> 00:00:26,834
A recursive formula gives each term from
earlier ones. The Fibonacci sequence

6
00:00:26,834 --> 00:00:31,183
starts one, one, and each term is the sum
of the previous two.

7
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:34,043
One, one, two, three, five, eight,
thirteen.

8
00:00:35,167 --> 00:00:39,455
Find a formula for one half, two thirds,
three fourths, four fifths.

9
00:00:39,905 --> 00:00:46,091
The top is n and the bottom is n plus one,
so a sub n equals n over n plus one.

10
00:00:47,233 --> 00:00:52,937
The sum from k equals one to five of k
squared is one plus four plus nine plus

11
00:00:52,937 --> 00:00:56,300
sixteen plus twenty five, which is fifty
five.

12
00:00:56,750 --> 00:01:02,151
Sums split: the sum of a plus b is the sum
of a plus the sum of b, and constants

13
00:01:02,151 --> 00:01:02,894
factor out.

14
00:01:04,033 --> 00:01:10,113
Two useful formulas: the sum of k from one
to n is n times n plus one over two.

15
00:01:10,563 --> 00:01:16,817
The sum of k squared is n times n plus one
times two n plus one, over six. For n

16
00:01:16,817 --> 00:01:20,334
equals ten, that's three hundred eighty
five.

17
00:01:21,467 --> 00:01:26,288
Your turn. Evaluate the sum from k equals
one to twenty of three k.

18
00:01:26,738 --> 00:01:28,274
Pause the video and try it.

19
00:01:28,724 --> 00:01:33,524
Three times twenty times twenty one over
two, which is six hundred thirty.

20
00:01:34,666 --> 00:01:40,014
In this lesson, we'll learn mathematical
induction, a way to prove a statement for

21
00:01:40,014 --> 00:01:41,514
every positive integer.

22
00:01:42,633 --> 00:01:47,966
Think of dominoes. If the first one falls,
and each falling domino knocks over the

23
00:01:47,966 --> 00:01:49,267
next, they all fall.

24
00:01:49,717 --> 00:01:54,197
Induction proves the first case, then
shows each case implies the next.

25
00:01:55,333 --> 00:02:01,756
Let's prove one plus three plus five, up
to two n minus one, equals n squared. Base

26
00:02:01,756 --> 00:02:03,226
case: n equals one.

27
00:02:03,676 --> 00:02:07,537
The left side is one and the right side is
one squared. True.

28
00:02:08,666 --> 00:02:14,585
Inductive step: assume it's true for n
equals k, so the sum up to two k minus one

29
00:02:14,585 --> 00:02:15,535
is k squared.

30
00:02:15,985 --> 00:02:22,196
Add the next odd number, two k plus one,
to both sides: the sum becomes k squared

31
00:02:22,196 --> 00:02:23,729
plus two k plus one.

32
00:02:24,866 --> 00:02:29,069
And k squared plus two k plus one is k
plus one squared.

33
00:02:29,519 --> 00:02:35,513
That's the statement for n equals k plus
one. So by induction, it holds for all n.

34
00:02:36,633 --> 00:02:42,250
Prove that two to the n is greater than n
for all n. Base case: two is greater than

35
00:02:42,250 --> 00:02:42,521
one.

36
00:02:42,971 --> 00:02:48,939
If two to the k is greater than k, then
two to the k plus one is greater than two

37
00:02:48,939 --> 00:02:51,739
k, which is at least k plus one. Done.

38
00:02:52,866 --> 00:02:58,088
Your turn. What's the base case for
proving the sum of the first n integers

39
00:02:58,088 --> 00:03:00,525
equals n times n plus one over two?

40
00:03:00,975 --> 00:03:02,511
Pause the video and try it.

41
00:03:02,961 --> 00:03:08,420
n equals one: the left side is one, and
the right side is one times two over two,

42
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:09,297
which is one.

43
00:03:10,433 --> 00:03:16,016
In this lesson, we'll expand powers like a
plus b to the fifth without multiplying it

44
00:03:16,016 --> 00:03:16,278
out.

45
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:22,498
Pascal's triangle starts with one. Each
entry is the sum of the two above it.

46
00:03:22,948 --> 00:03:25,508
Row four is one, four, six, four, one.

47
00:03:26,633 --> 00:03:33,582
These are binomial coefficients, n choose
k, equal to n factorial over k factorial

48
00:03:33,582 --> 00:03:35,785
times n minus k factorial.

49
00:03:36,235 --> 00:03:37,664
Four choose two is six.

50
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:45,311
The binomial theorem: a plus b to the n
equals the sum of n choose k times a to

51
00:03:45,311 --> 00:03:47,866
the n minus k times b to the k.

52
00:03:48,316 --> 00:03:51,559
The powers of a go down while the powers
of b go up.

53
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:54,513
Expand x plus two cubed.

54
00:03:54,963 --> 00:04:00,986
x cubed, plus three x squared times two,
plus three x times four, plus eight: x

55
00:04:00,986 --> 00:04:04,798
cubed plus six x squared plus twelve x
plus eight.

56
00:04:05,933 --> 00:04:10,114
Find the x to the fourth term of two x
minus one, to the seventh.

57
00:04:10,564 --> 00:04:16,054
That needs k equals three: seven choose
three times two x to the fourth times

58
00:04:16,054 --> 00:04:21,829
negative one cubed. That's thirty five
times sixteen times negative one: negative

59
00:04:21,829 --> 00:04:24,324
five hundred sixty x to the fourth.

60
00:04:25,466 --> 00:04:30,352
Your turn. Find the coefficient of x
squared in x plus three, to the fifth.

61
00:04:30,802 --> 00:04:32,338
Pause the video and try it.

62
00:04:32,788 --> 00:04:38,417
Five choose three times three cubed, which
is ten times twenty seven, or two hundred

63
00:04:38,417 --> 00:04:38,953
seventy.

64
00:04:40,100 --> 00:04:44,644
In this lesson, we'll pull together
counting and probability, ready for

65
00:04:44,644 --> 00:04:46,180
calculus and statistics.

66
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:51,823
Permutations count ordered arrangements;
combinations count groups.

67
00:04:52,273 --> 00:04:57,137
Seven P three is two hundred ten. Seven
choose three is thirty five.

68
00:04:58,267 --> 00:05:03,414
A committee of four is chosen from six
women and five men. What's the chance it's

69
00:05:03,414 --> 00:05:04,176
two of each?

70
00:05:04,626 --> 00:05:10,477
Six choose two times five choose two, over
eleven choose four: one fifty over three

71
00:05:10,477 --> 00:05:12,733
thirty, which is five elevenths.

72
00:05:13,867 --> 00:05:20,845
The binomial probability of exactly k
successes in n tries is n choose k times p

73
00:05:20,845 --> 00:05:24,683
to the k times one minus p to the n minus
k.

74
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:30,862
Exactly two heads in five flips: ten times
one half to the fifth, which is ten thirty

75
00:05:30,862 --> 00:05:32,749
seconds, or five sixteenths.

76
00:05:33,867 --> 00:05:38,240
Expected value is the sum of each outcome
times its probability.

77
00:05:38,690 --> 00:05:44,549
A game pays ten dollars with probability
one fifth and nothing otherwise, and costs

78
00:05:44,549 --> 00:05:50,338
three dollars to play. The expected value
is two minus three, negative one dollar.

79
00:05:51,467 --> 00:05:53,579
For at least one, use the complement.

80
00:05:54,029 --> 00:05:59,752
Rolling at least one six in four rolls:
one minus five sixths to the fourth, about

81
00:05:59,752 --> 00:06:01,218
point five one eight.

82
00:06:02,367 --> 00:06:06,100
Your turn. A test has ten true-false
questions. What's the probability of

83
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:08,980
guessing exactly eight right?

84
00:06:09,430 --> 00:06:10,966
Pause the video and try it.

85
00:06:11,416 --> 00:06:17,144
Ten choose eight over two to the tenth:
forty five over one thousand twenty four,

86
00:06:17,144 --> 00:06:19,053
about point zero four four.

